8. My recommended build sequence

Hindi ko sabay-sabay gagawin lahat. Ito ang sequence na magbibigay ng mas controlled na development.

Phase 1 — Content and architecture

Finalize the message, section order, terminology, CTA, and commercial boundaries.

Phase 2 — Design system and responsive shell

Build the typography, colors, navigation, layout, mobile drawer, and reusable components.

Phase 3 — Main website content

Implement Hero, Overview, Tracks, Tiers, Categories, Engagement, Process, Structure, and Fit.

Phase 4 — Trust and conversion

Add FAQ, accurate policy links, contact workflow, validation, success states, and fallback contact options.

Phase 5 — Backend and admin

Add content management, inquiry records, access control, audit logs, and publishing workflow only where needed.

Phase 6 — QA and launch

Test accessibility, responsive layouts, performance, browser compatibility, security, and all primary user journeys.

My final recommendation

For your AIP website, I would build one premium, long-form partner experience with a reusable design system, clear information architecture, reliable CTA, and a lightweight admin/content layer.

The essential launch scope would be:

Brand system

Responsive navigation

Hero and value proposition

Overview

Contribution areas

Tier hierarchy

Category explanation

Commercial models

Partner process

Structure

Transparency and risk

Fit check

Contact workflow

Privacy/accessibility

QA and monitoring

Then add advanced features only after the core experience is working.

The most important principle: Huwag nating gawing “10/10” dahil maraming sections, effects, or technology. Gawin natin itong 10/10 dahil clear ang message, organized ang system, trustworthy ang claims, usable sa mobile, and functional ang journey from discovery to actual partnership.
